d3d11: move a shared surface's pixels on the thread that owns them

Patch 0008 gives each participant in a MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX surface its own
texture and carries the content between them at the keyed-mutex handoff.  It
did both halves of that carry in AcquireSync: the acquiring thread issued
CopyResource and Map against the *releasing* device's immediate context.

That is a context another thread owns.  D3D11 immediate contexts are
single-threaded by contract; Wine's global lock keeps it from corrupting
anything, which is why it worked at all, and it does not order the copy against
the work the owning thread has already queued -- so the copy could read a
texture the owner had not finished drawing into.

Measured, one Word start-up with +d3d11 (the thread id is the log's own prefix):

    thread 01a4   owns device 7E18118DB140, acquires/releases the three
                  2048x1280 owner textures
    thread 01c0   owns device 7FFFFF745488, acquires/releases the three
                  textures opened from them

    01c0  Moved 2048x1280 from 7E17702B73B0 -> 7FFFFF867330   x10
    01c0  Moved 2048x1280 from 7E1770209650 -> 7FFFFF867470   x10
    01c0  Moved 2048x1280 from 7E17702B7310 -> 7FFFFF867830   x9
    01a4  Moved 2048x1280 from 7FFFFF867470 -> 7E1770209650   x9
    01a4  Moved 2048x1280 from 7FFFFF867330 -> 7E17702B73B0   x9
    01a4  Moved 2048x1280 from 7FFFFF867830 -> 7E17702B7310   x8

Every one of the 55 handoffs in that start-up reads a texture from the wrong
thread.  What it looks like from the outside: Word's Backstage start screen
comes up with its title bar (rows 1..47) and its navigation rail (columns
1..65) composited **solid black**, while the Backstage content between them is
correct -- Office composites its UI out of those three shared atlases, so an
atlas that arrives incomplete blacks out exactly the elements it carried.  It
is a race, so it is intermittent, and it is invisible in the two conditions
this project usually measures in:

    hardware GL (radeonsi), untraced       2 of 6 starts had a black band
    hardware GL with +d3d11 tracing        0 of 1
    LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1                0 of 1

Resizing the window makes the band come back correctly, which is what says the
content was never lost -- only one composite got it wrong and that frame stuck.

**What this patch does not do: it does not make the band go away.**  The first
fourteen starts after it were clean, which read as a fix and was written up as
one.  It did not hold:

    before                       2 of 6   starts had a black band
    after, the first fourteen    0 of 14
    after, the next eleven       5 of 11

-- 5 of 25 overall, against 2 of 6 before, which is no effect these numbers can
show.  The fourteen-run stretch has no explanation.  The one candidate that
looked worth testing -- that the boundary is the rebuild which added the 0016
and 0017 diagnostics, guarded off but still changing code layout in the two
functions involved -- was tested: with both reverted, the band reproduces
again within two starts.  So that is not it either.

The patch is still right and still worth carrying, on its own terms rather
than on a symptom: issuing `CopyResource` and `Map` against a device's
immediate context from a thread that does not own it is not defensible under
D3D11's threading contract whatever the pixels do, it happened on every one of
the 55 handoffs in a start-up, and the fix costs nothing -- the same two copies
happen, on the correct side of each.  What it means for the black band is that
the cause is still open, and that whoever picks it up should not start from
"the handoff was fixed, so it must be something else".

The fix is not a lock or a flush; it is doing each half of the carry on the
thread that is allowed to do it.  ReleaseSync copies the releasing texture out
into a system-memory buffer on the shared state -- it runs on the releasing
device's own thread, after that thread's draws, and while the releaser still
holds the surface so nobody can read the buffer half-written.  AcquireSync
copies the buffer into the acquiring texture on the acquiring device's own
thread.  A generation counter on the surface, mirrored on each texture, skips
the upload when a participant already holds the current pixels; that also
replaces the old "content" pointer, which was the only reason one texture ever
had to name another.

One behaviour changes with it, and it is worth stating rather than hiding:
0008 seeded the surface with the owner's texture as its content, so a newly
opened texture could be composited before the owner had released anything.  A
system-memory buffer cannot be seeded that way -- filling it would mean reading
the owner's texture from the opener's thread, which is the bug this patch
removes.  Nothing regresses in practice because the keyed-mutex protocol
already orders it: the surface starts on key 0, only the owner acquires that,
so the owner's first release always precedes any other participant's acquire.

--- a/dlls/d3d11/shared.c
+++ b/dlls/d3d11/shared.c
@@ -17,6 +17,21 @@
  * That costs a round trip through system memory per handoff.  It is the honest
  * price of not having shared storage, and it is paid only by surfaces an
  * application actually shares.
+ *
+ * The half of that round trip that reads a texture is done in ReleaseSync and
+ * the half that writes one in AcquireSync, so that each of them runs on the
+ * thread that owns the device it touches.  The first version of this file did
+ * both halves at AcquireSync, which meant the acquiring thread issued
+ * CopyResource and Map against the *releasing* device's immediate context --
+ * a context that belongs to another thread.  D3D11 immediate contexts are
+ * single-threaded by contract; Wine's global lock stops that from corrupting
+ * anything, and it does not order the copy against the work the owning thread
+ * has already queued, so the copy could read a texture the owner had not
+ * finished drawing.  Measured under Word: three 2048x1280 atlases, 55 handoffs
+ * per start-up, every one of them crossing threads that way, and about one
+ * start-up in three came up with a whole group of UI elements -- the title bar,
+ * the Backstage navigation rail -- composited solid black.  Hardware GL only:
+ * under llvmpipe, and under +d3d11 tracing, it never reproduced.
  */
 
 #include "d3d11_private.h"
@@ -32,7 +47,13 @@
     UINT64 key;
     BOOL acquired;
 
-    struct d3d_texture2d *content;  /* whose pixels are current, NULL if nobody has written */
+    /* The current pixels.  A texture cannot be the carrier: it belongs to one
+     * device, and reading it from another device's thread is the bug this
+     * file's header describes.  System memory belongs to nobody, so both
+     * halves of the handoff can run on the right thread. */
+    void *bits;
+    UINT pitch, rows, generation;
+
     struct list textures;
 };
 
